The Tribonacci sequence function `tri(n)` generates the first `n + 1` numbers in the Tribonacci sequence. It first handles base cases for `n = 0`, `n = 1`, and `n = 2`, which are special because they don't follow the general recurrence rule. 

For larger `n`, it initializes the sequence with the first three numbers `[1, 3, 2]` and then iterates from 3 to `n`, appending the appropriate value to the sequence based on whether `i` (the index in the sequence) is even or odd. When `i` is even, it uses the formula `tri(n) = 1 + n / 2`. When `i` is odd, it calculates the value as the sum of the previous three numbers in the sequence: `tri(n - 1) + tri(n - 2) + tri(n - 3)`.

Finally, the function returns the generated list of Tribonacci numbers up to `n + 1`.

